WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.960
Help all kids learn and grow with PBS Kids.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.240
Thank you for supporting your PBS station.

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:11.650
We're here in North America, down in the Southwest,

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.060
in a habitat called the Sonoran Desert.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.540
Hey, it's us, the Krat Brothers.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:18.680
I'm Martin.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:19.520
I'm Chris.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.280
And out here in this desert, the earth is dry and sandy,

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.900
and the landscape is dotted by these awesome

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.220
saguaro cactuses.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.980
It seems like it could be really tough to make

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.880
a home out here but if you look and listen closely check it out a gila woodpecker has made a hole

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.880
in that saguaro and that is where the gila woodpecker lives but when the woodpecker's done

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:49.090
with that hole other animals can move in and in the more grassy areas ground squirrels and prairie

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:55.690
dogs make homes and tunnels underground and those burrows and tunnels can be homes for other animals

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.430
like Gila Monsters, Rattlesnakes...

00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.970
Uh, Chris, I think you forgot some...

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.530
and burrowing owls.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.670
Can you believe it?

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.220
An owl that lives underground.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.720
And one of the smallest owls in the world.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.600
A little desert elf.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.880
This tiny owl is only about the size of my hand

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.740
and weighs about as much as a small orange.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.530
It's one of the few small species of owl

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.430
that call this desert home.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:25.880
Even though they're small,

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.900
burrowing owls are fierce hunters.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:29.980
They'll eat just about anything they can kill.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.840
those talents from beetles and grasshoppers to mice and snakes birds and

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.380
even young ground squirrels that actually built their homes in the first

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:44.390
place and their amazing owl powers of sight and hearing are the keys to their

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:49.580
hunting success imagine if we had the sensory powers of an owl then we'd

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:53.640
really get an insider's view into the lives of the desert elves

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:03.230
You

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.380
and lizard glide, falcon flight, and lion ride.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:47.630
Blizzard L.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.590
Cut it out! You're acting like a crap brother!

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:27.660
Now and then, I like getting out and letting loose on the hover bike!

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:42.100
You know desert creeps me out the blazing heat hardly any water the creepy crawlies

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:49.010
See, it's freaky. Let's just get to town get those supplies. We need and get back to the tortuga

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.480
Why are you collecting them anyway, Jimmy?

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.560
They go well with my Pebble collection.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.440
Besides, they're cute.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:15.740
Can't do it right now, guys.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.760
They'll be back from the supply run by noon,

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.980
and they have a lot of cleaning up to do.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:22.500
They're everywhere.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:29.540
You just got them.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.820
Oh, brothers! Not the begging! Anything but the begging!

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:35.140
Please!

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:38.620
Ugh!

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:43.780
Kay, just get... I can't take it! Sheesh!

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.320
So, Xaviva?

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.660
I know you guys have been wanting this power since we got to the Sonoran Desert, but hurry up and get me some info!

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.650
I can't make a suit without knowing exactly how the creature power works!

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:58.910
Don't worry! We've got a plan!

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.880
Steady. Steady.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.650
Move it out, Martin!

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.420
I can't see anything down here.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.310
Okay, just a little to the right and we're there.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:16.040
Whoa! Whoa! Whoa!

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.080
A little more to the right. Yep, almost there.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:19.540
Where?

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:22.190
Too much!

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:26.530
Stop! There! The cactus with eyes!

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:29.310
Okay, a few steps to the left.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:35.360
We did it! We're close to an elf owl!

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.910
The smallest owl in the world!

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:40.930
What a great place for a nest!

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.270
It's a really cool place away from the hot sun.

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:46.430
And with all those cactus spines,

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:50.750
it's a tough place for predators like coyotes and bobcats to get to.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:57.050
Heather!

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.970
Okay, we just need to get a scan of the elf owl.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.940
and we'll have what Evieva needs to make the power suit.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:04.200
I can't wait!

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.180
The eyesight, the hearing, the stealth flight.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:08.560
Oh, we've got to get it all.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.910
Owl Powers, here we go.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.310
Ha-ha! We did it!

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:35.170
Right! I got it, bro! Wait, no!

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.340
Why'd you say right, not right?

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:52.310
All right. As in, I got the feather.

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:53.920
Do? Mm-hmm.

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:58.490
Well, I did have the feather.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:00.620
Aw.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:10.370
Oh, great.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:12.790
Please tell me this is a joke.

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:14.330
Hang on.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.210
Kay, I'm a very powerful.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:21.660
Isn't this kind of stuff supposed to happen

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.240
to the Krat Brothers and not us?

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:28.560
You mean being, being stranded way out here in the desert?

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:29.980
Well, it's not suppo-

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.240
to me. No water, no food, no communication.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:42.070
Remember, the Sonoran Desert has more

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:46.380
rainfall than any other desert. We'll be fine. But it's still

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:50.740
a desert. It might not rain for days. And we can't last that long without

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:55.910
water. Well, we'll just stay put. Aviva and the Krabbrothers know

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:59.790
we were heading towards town. They'll realize we're missing and come looking for us.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:04.000
Problem is, you decided to take the long, scenic route, remember?

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:05.790
They'll find us.

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:08.530
We're in this together, little beanbag.

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:16.840
1,356?

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:20.510
2,357?

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:24.760
Uh, we gotta stop running into you like this.

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:28.880
Oh, great!

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:29.970
We find...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.620
out you like to sit on people's heads.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:35.100
Would have been a lot less painful if we'd known that before.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:36.390
Ow!

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:40.020
At least now we can get a good look at this little elf owl's features.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:42.320
Like those eyes. They're huge!

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.130
That's right! I'm checking out the scan you got.

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:52.290
And according to my analysis, owls have eyes that are up to 100 times better at seeing in the dark than ours are.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:56.160
Yeah, and even though they can't move them around like we can,

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.680
they can turn their heads completely backward.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:03.240
Like, ow, like we can't do.

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:06.900
That's how it's done.

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:11.730
Great, that's owl sensory power number one, eyesight.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:13.640
Now let's check out that hearing.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:15.660
We need owl-powered ears.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.060
Elphys here can show us that too.

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:20.820
Elvis? Like the famous singer?

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:24.120
No, Elphys. Elphys the Elf Owl.

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:25.390
That's what I'm naming him.

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:27.350
Hold everything!

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:29.470
Huh? Ow!

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:31.890
Missed one. What's up, Aviva?

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:34.670
I just realized Cokie and Jimmy aren't back yet.

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:36.020
They're super late.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:37.960
Well, just call them.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:40.110
I did, no answer.

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:42.670
Oh, Cokie's the communication queen.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.270
She wouldn't be late without calling.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:45.780
What if something went wrong?

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:48.950
Ah, okay, well, just keep trying to reach them.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:51.990
We'll put the Elf Owl Adventure on hold and be right there.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.480
Sorry, Elphas. Emergency.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:56.950
We'll catch up with you later.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:11.330
Oh, it sure is scorching hot out here.

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:13.370
Not a good place to be stranded.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:15.640
Yeah, we need to find them fast.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:17.020
Guys, over here!

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:20.360
Aren't these the seeds Jimmy was collecting?

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:22.810
Yes, they're driving me crazy.

00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:25.170
He's been leaving them lying around everywhere.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:25.790
Hmm?

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:27.700
Exactly.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:29.850
He leaves the seeds everywhere.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:34.420
And look, they left a whole trail of seeds.

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:37.510
Huh, he spilled them as they were riding off.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:40.760
So if we follow the trail of seeds...

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:43.350
It should lead us right to Jimmy and Koki.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:45.280
Yes! Woo-hoo!

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:49.780
We'll have Koki and Jimmy back here in no time.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:51.270
Follow those seeds!

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.050
The sea trail is petering out.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:15.310
That's strange. Back here, it's fine.

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:19.510
But wait, they were here, now they're gone.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.120
But that doesn't make sense.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:23.400
We just looked this way for a second.

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:26.250
Huh? Now there are even less.

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:29.650
What? Aha!

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:33.040
Jumping Beans!

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:35.210
But look, some don't jump.

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:37.860
Why do those jump and these don't jump?

00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:39.810
Come on, Lise Bean, jump!

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:41.190
Let's see!

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:45.020
Hey, get back here!

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:47.800
Uh-oh!

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:48.700
Don't move!

00:11:49.460 --> 00:11:50.660
It's not a good sound.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:53.480
Yeah, I'd say a rattlesnake rattling at my hand

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:54.760
is definitely not good.

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:57.460
Especially if he stops making the warning sound

00:11:57.460 --> 00:11:58.840
and decides to strike.

00:11:59.410 --> 00:11:59.690
Yeah.

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:01.610
That would be even more not good.

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:03.570
I'll see if I can coax him out.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.360
Hey, Rattlesnake, we didn't mean to bug you.

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:11.140
We just, Chris, it's in.

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:14.480
I'm done.

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:18.270
The venom of the rattler is coursing through my veins.

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:19.250
I can feel it.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:23.790
Uh, Chris, wait a sec.

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:24.670
Can't wait.

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:25.910
I don't have much longer.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:28.000
Goodbye, brother of mine.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:29.500
I'm feeling weak.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:32.480
Uh, Chris, it wasn't a rattlesnake.

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:34.580
It was an owl. Huh?

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:44.610
Guy. Yes, another desert elf.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:46.100
And this one lives underground.

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:50.860
And is also happy to be out in the daytime.

00:12:51.140 --> 00:12:53.740
Who said our owl adventure was on hold?

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:58.150
Oh, they use old ground squirrel holes as their homes.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:12:59.980
And so do...

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:03.820
So, any one of the holes out here could have a ground squirrel,

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.710
a rattlesnake, or a burrowing owl living in it.

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:08.500
So, if you're a little burrowing owl

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:11.140
with some big predator poking around your home,

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:13.420
who better to imitate than a rattlesnake?

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:15.200
Uh-oh.

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:19.390
That coyote is looking for a meal.

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:23.810
Question is, who's down that hole?

00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:26.510
I'm betting that one really is a rattler.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:28.410
I'm going with the burrowing owl.

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:32.180
Ooh, back down.

00:13:32.890 --> 00:13:34.620
Yes! A burrowing owl!

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:36.700
Now that's a great trick.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:39.040
Mimicry burrowing owl style.

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:42.130
They got us and a coyote to back off.

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:44.300
Plus, I got the jumping bean back.

00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:47.390
Viva here, come in.

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:49.580
Did you locate Koki and Jimmy?

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:52.110
No, but I've been checking out these jumping beans,

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:54.420
and you won't believe what's making them jump.

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:57.400
Uh, cool new basketball sneakers?

00:13:58.860 --> 00:13:59.700
Not exactly.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:03.440
Look inside. It's a caterpillar.

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:07.120
And when the caterpillar flips around inside the seed, it jumps.

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:11.410
So if the seed has a caterpillar inside, it's a jumping bean.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:14.580
If it doesn't, it's just a regular seed that doesn't jump.

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:18.520
But why? Why flip around and jump in the first place?

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:22.940
Look at this. Caterpillars don't like getting too hot.

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:26.060
So they jump around until they find a cooler spot.

00:14:26.590 --> 00:14:29.430
Ah, so that's why we're losing Jimmy and Cokie's trail.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.700
It's the heat of the day and the seeds with the caterpillars,

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:36.140
the jumping beans, are jumping around to get away from the heat.

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:38.120
Oh, if too many seeds jump away,

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:40.560
the seed trail could disappear completely.

00:14:42.250 --> 00:14:44.080
No time to lose. Let's go!

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:46.490
We're coming, Cokie and Jimmy Z.

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:52.370
You getting hungry, Cokie?

00:14:52.530 --> 00:14:54.970
I think I'm okay. For now.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:59.870
Not me. All I can think about is my little mini fridge, packed.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.800
with all my favorite snacks.

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:06.540
Hot dogs, popcorn, pepperoni pizza.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.020
I think you're getting a little delirious.

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:10.710
You need some food and water.

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:13.130
I hope those guys are looking for us,

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:14.730
because we're frying out here.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:19.060
This trail goes all over the place.

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:21.780
It's like they were taking the long way to town or something.

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:25.210
Whoa! A burrowing owl!

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:27.520
Oh, got a collared lizard!

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:29.870
These burrowing owls are masters.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.300
and catching insects, lizards, small mammals, and...

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:34.580
Hey, that's pretty amazing!

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:36.920
Nothing like a cool creature distraction,

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:38.800
as we follow the seed trail.

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:41.920
Hey, get back here with that seed!

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:44.010
Don't you mess up our trail!

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:51.180
Stop! It's all this stuff at his doorstep.

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:56.180
Shells, feathers, seeds, ooh, even mammal dung.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:57.880
A beetle!

00:15:58.550 --> 00:15:59.970
Amazing! You out...

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.760
Owls don't only hunt, you attract food right to your doorstep.

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:07.680
These beetles eat dung, and they like to live and hide under all kinds of things.

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:10.500
So the beetles come here thinking, oh, it's a great place to be,

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:13.230
and then you owls gobble them up.

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:15.310
Hey, but just don't take those seats, okay?

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:17.780
We need that sea trail to follow our friends.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:20.960
Guys, did you look at the sun?

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:22.820
No, what, huh?

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:24.510
It's going down.

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:28.590
That means it'll be dark in just a few minutes.

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:29.970
Too dark to see the...

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:35.710
Too dark to find Cokie and Jimmy.

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:36.570
Hurry!

00:16:42.350 --> 00:16:44.930
Too late. I can't see anything now.

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:50.430
Martin, look, it's Elphys.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:51.880
Elphys!

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:54.940
Someone's having no trouble seeing.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:56.120
Are you kidding?

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:58.840
He could probably see us a mile away with those eyes.

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:02.350
Those eyes! Owl eyes!

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:04.890
Owls are the masters of the night!

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:08.160
With incredible night vision and excellent hearing!

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:13.400
Owlbrick can help us save Cokie and Jimmy!

00:17:13.810 --> 00:17:16.430
The answer was right under our noses the whole time.

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:19.160
Aviva, come in!

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:20.690
I'm already on it, guys.

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:24.370
Having a look at owl sensory feature number two now, hearing.

00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:26.770
When a sound comes in,

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:29.970
the big, round, disc-shaped face of the owl works like a...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:33.260
a huge satellite dish, collecting as much of the sound as possible.

00:17:33.560 --> 00:17:33.940
Wow!

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:37.680
And not only that, owl ears are hidden under their feathers.

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:42.450
But one ear is way down here, and one ear is way up here.

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:46.060
That helps the owl pinpoint exactly where the sound is coming from.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:49.040
Amazing. If you can get those features in a suit,

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:51.880
they'll really help us track down Cokie and Jimmy Z.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:55.320
Okay, but whatever you do, don't lose Alphys.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:57.230
We need his help with the transformation.

00:17:57.950 --> 00:17:59.590
Don't worry, we've got that covered.

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:04.410
We're sticking with you, pal. We need your help tonight.

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:06.680
Do you hear something? See something?

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:10.060
I bet those ears and eyes are in full hunt mode right now.

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:11.920
I don't hear or see a thing.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:15.260
But an owl can hear an insect skittling in the leaf litter

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:17.240
from 75 feet away.

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:22.070
It's heading your way!

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:25.890
Wait up, buddy! What? You heard something way over there?

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:36.850
Incredible! You knew that scorpion was hiding there from way over in that tree!

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:40.080
Those are exactly the kind of senses we need!

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:43.980
Well, those are the kind of senses you're getting right now!

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:46.620
The elf-owl power discs are ready to go!

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:48.500
Oh-ho! Just in time!

00:18:48.940 --> 00:18:51.200
I don't know how much longer we could have kept up with Elphus!

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:55.020
Two power discs ready for teleportation!

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:56.330
Coming at you, Jay-Z!

00:18:56.910 --> 00:18:57.430
Oops.

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:01.960
Oh, yeah, he's not here.

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:05.060
It's just not the same without Jimmy.

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:07.290
Zap it!

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.220
Activate Elf Owl Power!

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:23.040
The Koki Rescue!

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:28.340
Now this is what Nocturnal Search and Locate is all about.

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:29.980
This iGear...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:36.960
is awesome. I can see the sea trails so clearly now, even at night.

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:40.230
Ah, all right, all right. No need to scream, Chris.

00:19:40.610 --> 00:19:42.850
This owl hearing is ultra sensitive.

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:46.260
We're like nocturnal spy missiles.

00:19:46.750 --> 00:19:48.310
Come on, trail goes this way.

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:51.560
Just hope we're not too late.

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:00.940
Hear what?

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:02.130
Growl!

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:04.120
Something's out there.

00:20:05.830 --> 00:20:06.750
There it is again.

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:10.350
Jimmy, do you know what that growl is coming from?

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:11.760
I don't want to know.

00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:12.980
Your stomach.

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:19.260
Oh, well, that's because I'm starving out here.

00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:21.460
Hold it together a little longer, Jay-Z.

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:22.910
Those guys will find us.

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:24.530
They better find us fast.

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:26.350
Huh?

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:29.690
What's going on?

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:30.860
My seeds!

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:32.060
What?

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:33.040
Moth?

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:38.180
Chris, we're picking up some strange movements ahead.

00:20:38.680 --> 00:20:39.640
Wait a second.

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:40.780
Our trail!

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:42.550
It's hatching!

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:43.740
Whoa!

00:20:44.140 --> 00:20:48.690
Those little caterpillars inside the jumping beans transformed into moths!

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:51.410
They're leaving the seeds!

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:54.940
And check out Alphys!

00:20:55.100 --> 00:20:55.920
He loves it!

00:20:56.080 --> 00:20:58.250
He's a master insect hunter!

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:04.040
Wait, what's over there? Owl eyes, maximum focus.

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:11.660
More moths. Hey, if Jimmy had a whole bag of these jumping beans,

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:15.020
then that big swarm of moths could be from his bag.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:19.750
Oh, that's a lot of moths.

00:21:20.530 --> 00:21:24.010
Phew, jumping down somewhere, Jay-Z. Getting everywhere.

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:35.200
We're in trouble now!

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:38.030
How will they ever find us way down here?

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:41.390
And there's no food down here either.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:47.910
Look, it's Jimmy's bag of jumping beans.

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:50.420
Oh, but no sign of Jimmy and Cokie.

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:53.340
Hmm, maybe they ditched the seeds for some reason.

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:55.140
That means our trail ends here.

00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:57.200
Yeah, but we know they were heading that way.

00:21:57.840 --> 00:21:59.900
So let's just keep on searching in the direction.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:00.820
they were heading.

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:02.070
Good idea, bro.

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:05.870
And keep those owl senses tuned in.

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:08.280
Wait, what's that?

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:11.410
Owl hearing maximum power.

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:16.240
It's Jimmy's stomach.

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:19.360
That's the sound it makes when he's hungry for pizza.

00:22:19.870 --> 00:22:21.550
Quick, it's coming from over there.

00:22:21.830 --> 00:22:23.850
Oh, almost flew right past him.

00:22:24.850 --> 00:22:26.690
This owl power is incredible.

00:22:27.070 --> 00:22:29.970
Being able to pinpoint a tiny sound like that...

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:32.330
and hone in on exactly where it's coming from.

00:22:32.660 --> 00:22:34.640
Let's just hope ours works as good as his.

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:40.730
Ha ha ha! Jimmy!

00:22:42.340 --> 00:22:50.190
How'd you do it?

00:22:50.570 --> 00:22:53.930
Let's just say we got some help from a little desert elf

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:56.360
with big creature powers.

00:23:02.330 --> 00:23:03.410
And gotcha!

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:06.880
Mission accomplished!

00:23:07.370 --> 00:23:08.970
We got Jimmy and Koki back!

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:12.070
Thanks to my jumping bean collection.

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:14.710
Which we now know are really caterpillars

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:17.030
that live inside the seed of a Sebastiana bush.

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:20.470
And thanks to the desert elves of the Sonoran Desert.

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:24.040
Their creature powers of sight and hearing are incredible.

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:26.450
Don't forget Jimmy's rumbly tummy.

00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:29.970
Yeah, it's a good thing my tummy called out for pizza when it...

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.320
Right, guys?

00:23:32.230 --> 00:23:32.570
Chris?

00:23:33.230 --> 00:23:33.570
Martin?

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:34.610
Uh-oh.

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:36.980
Don't tell me we lost somebody else now.

00:23:37.360 --> 00:23:38.200
They're not lost.

00:23:38.490 --> 00:23:42.800
Look, I guess after a long night of looking and listening,

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:44.420
every owl needs some shut-eye.

00:23:45.560 --> 00:23:49.270
So the desert elves,

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:52.190
both the elf owl that lives in the saguaro cactus

00:23:52.190 --> 00:23:54.810
and the burrowing owl that lives underground,

00:23:55.250 --> 00:23:57.990
are little creatures with big sensory powers.

00:23:58.230 --> 00:23:59.970
But both of these owls will also have a...

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:03.850
big problem every year there are fewer and fewer of them and they're

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:08.780
disappearing from parts of North America so we're teaming up with our friend Brit

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:14.120
from the University of Arizona hey Brit hey how's it going Brit and her team

00:24:14.120 --> 00:24:19.060
are trying to learn more about one of these owls the burrowing owls yeah this

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:24.900
is a burrowing owl burrow one of the ways you can tell is by all this strange

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:29.560
mammal dung and other things right at the entrance of the den the owls carry

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:35.060
dung here. It attracts beetles and other insects to the den so that they can eat

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:39.560
food right at their doorstep. What we're gonna do is set up a special owl trap

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:43.180
so that we can get a close look at these owls and find out more information

00:24:43.180 --> 00:24:49.560
about them. The trap's set. Now we wait. And so with a cool little elf owl in a

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:56.960
nearby tree we waited, waited, until it worked. We caught one. One of the pair of

00:24:56.960 --> 00:24:59.980
burrowing owls. When he's in my hand like this you can really see

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:07.180
how tiny this owl is and incredibly lightweight so first thing we do is take measurements and weigh

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:13.750
them and then banding each bird gets a lightweight band that gives it a unique identification number

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:19.820
they're so light the owl will hardly even know they're there being able to identify each individual

00:25:19.820 --> 00:25:26.400
owl helps brit learn new things about owls for example she now knows that burrowing owls often

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:29.820
use the same burrow year after year we've also learned that

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:31.950
Some burrowing owls migrate and some don't.

00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:33.000
So which one are you?

00:25:33.330 --> 00:25:34.470
Migrate or stay put?

00:25:34.830 --> 00:25:35.850
We'll find out.

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:37.970
Because we're ready to release the owl.

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:38.660
All right.

00:25:39.250 --> 00:25:43.510
Now that we've got all the information we need to help these owls, we're ready to let

00:25:43.510 --> 00:25:43.890
them go.

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.460
Back to living free and in the wild.

00:25:47.620 --> 00:25:52.400
And with the help of a motion-sensitive camera, we can watch and study them as our pair of

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:54.080
owls raise some chicks of their own.

00:25:54.950 --> 00:25:57.070
And we'll see you again soon on the Creature Trail.

00:25:57.350 --> 00:25:58.630
Keep on creature adventuring.

00:25:58.790 --> 00:25:59.210
Awesome.

00:26:17.630 --> 00:26:22.270
about cool animals and collect your own Wild Kratz creature powers,

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:26.550
go to the Wild Kratz website at pbskids.org.

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:28.250
We'll see you there!
